id: pm-j0w7j9
title: "Declared activation.commands is silently overridden by the renderers/hooks/parser/preflight capability tier, so any extension beyond plain commands is eagerly imported on every invocation"
description: "extensionNeedsActivationForProbe (src/cli/main.ts) evaluates GLOBAL_EXTENSION_ACTIVATION_CAPABILITIES = {hooks, parser, preflight, renderers} BEFORE the 'commands.length > 0 -> return false' early exit. Consequence: once an extension declares any one of those four capabilities, its own declared activation.commands scope has no effect at all and the extension is imported plus activated for every command probe. Only an extension declaring exclusively command-bearing capabilities can ever be skipped.\n\nProven in a clean sandbox with the SHIPPED scaffold, not by reading code. 'pm extension --init ./rend-ext --capability renderers' generates capabilities [commands, renderers] and activation.commands [\"rend ext ping\"] - a narrow, exact scope. After installing it, 'pm list --limit 1 --profile' reports 'profile:extensions loaded=1 ... renderer_overrides=1': the extension was imported and activated for a command that is provably not in its declared scope. The sibling commands-only scaffold in the same workspace was correctly skipped. The scaffold therefore emits a manifest field that is inert for the capability it was generated for, which is worse than emitting nothing - an author reads the generated activation block as a promise the runtime does not keep.\n\nMeasured cost at HEAD in this repository (14 installed extensions, best-of-5, node dist/cli.js list --limit 5): 784 ms with extensions vs 392 ms with --no-extensions = 2.00x. pm-4oww closed on the acceptance criterion 'warm per-command latency with all bundled packages installed within 1.5x of bare workspace'. That criterion is violated at HEAD and no gate measures it, so the regression is invisible. Four of the fourteen installed manifests (builtin-digital-twin-exemplar, builtin-kanban-profile, builtin-lifecycle-hooks, builtin-vcs-exemplar) declare no activation block at all and fall through the conservative tier; the single managed npm extension in this repo (pm-changelog) declares renderers and is therefore pinned eager by this defect.\n\nThis is the structural reason 'extremely extensible' and 'everything feels instant' currently trade against each other: the four capabilities an ambitious extension most wants (renderers, hooks, parser, preflight) are exactly the four that force it to tax every command. The tier is not an oversight in isolation - pm-halx decided the analogous schema tier deliberately - but no item owns the renderers/hooks/parser/preflight half, and unlike schema these four have a natural static scope (which command's output, which lifecycle phase, which parser target) that the manifest simply has no vocabulary to express.\n\nRepro: pm extension --init ./x --capability renderers; pm extension --install --project ./x; pm list --limit 1 --profile"
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 1
tags[7]: activation,agent-ux,"area:extensions","area:sdk",extensibility,perf,startup
created_at: "2026-07-28T10:27:34.772Z"
